1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,324
You're outside, and it's dark.

2
00:00:01,591 --> 00:00:02,635
Look up. Pick a star.

3
00:00:02,728 --> 00:00:05,712
Any one you like. Now answer me: how far
away is your star?

4
00:00:06,132 --> 00:00:08,036
Nothing comes. You're not alone in that.

5
00:00:08,303 --> 00:00:10,277
More than three thousand people got the
same question.

6
00:00:10,544 --> 00:00:13,156
One in four put the stars closer to us
than the Sun.

7
00:00:13,737 --> 00:00:16,685
Five out of six students wouldn't put a
number on the nearest one at all.

8
00:00:16,860 --> 00:00:19,390
Of those who tried, one in five landed in
the right range.

9
00:00:19,495 --> 00:00:20,946
About three people in a hundred.

10
00:00:21,366 --> 00:00:23,200
So there you stand in the street with no
answer.

11
00:00:23,885 --> 00:00:25,070
What you need is a triangle.

12
00:00:25,650 --> 00:00:27,264
Here is how you build yours.

13
00:00:27,684 --> 00:00:30,169
Put your thumb up, arm straight out, and
shut your left eye.

14
00:00:30,668 --> 00:00:31,759
Now swap eyes.

15
00:00:32,258 --> 00:00:34,905
Your thumb jumped. The wall stayed where
it was.

16
00:00:35,405 --> 00:00:36,496
Your two eyes sit apart.

17
00:00:36,670 --> 00:00:38,771
Each one looks past your thumb from its
own spot.

18
00:00:39,352 --> 00:00:40,826
Two spots, one jump.

19
00:00:41,247 --> 00:00:43,047
Now you need two spots much further apart.

20
00:00:43,279 --> 00:00:44,579
Stand in this street in January.

21
00:00:44,672 --> 00:00:46,727
Come back in July and stand on the same
flagstone.

22
00:00:47,098 --> 00:00:48,132
You never lifted a foot.

23
00:00:48,712 --> 00:00:50,825
And you have moved three hundred million
kilometres.

24
00:00:51,197 --> 00:00:52,197
Your planet carried you.

25
00:00:52,694 --> 00:00:55,330
Photograph a near star from both ends of
your ride, and it jumps.

26
00:00:55,504 --> 00:00:56,990
Same as your thumb did.

27
00:00:57,410 --> 00:00:59,929
A man in Königsberg spent two years
catching that jump.

28
00:01:00,510 --> 00:01:03,238
The same trick you just played against
your wall, done properly.

29
00:01:03,819 --> 00:01:06,419
His telescope had its main lens sawn clean
in half.

30
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:08,439
One half slid on a fine screw.

31
00:01:08,939 --> 00:01:12,050
Night after night he turned the screw
until two images of one faint star lay

32
00:01:12,050 --> 00:01:13,675
exactly on top of each other.

33
00:01:14,360 --> 00:01:15,963
Then he read the screw off by lamplight.

34
00:01:16,648 --> 00:01:17,867
That reading was the jump.

35
00:01:18,366 --> 00:01:21,919
Half of it, drawn as an angle, is what you
were after: the parallax.

36
00:01:22,012 --> 00:01:23,150
Small jump, far star.

37
00:01:23,359 --> 00:01:25,228
That is the whole rule, and now you have
it too.

38
00:01:25,647 --> 00:01:29,594
One catch. Between idea and lamplit
reading lie two hundred and ninety-five

39
00:01:30,015 --> 00:01:32,616
In 1543 a book set the Earth moving.

40
00:01:33,045 --> 00:01:34,438
The objection came at once.

41
00:01:34,938 --> 00:01:37,736
If you ride a moving platform, the stars
should sway.

42
00:01:38,061 --> 00:01:39,117
Nobody saw any sway.

43
00:01:39,384 --> 00:01:41,962
The sharpest eyes of the century belonged
to a Danish noble.

44
00:01:42,287 --> 00:01:44,748
Stand beside him at his quadrant — brass,
taller than you.

45
00:01:44,841 --> 00:01:49,613
No telescope. He charted the whole sky
with his bare eye, finer than anyone

46
00:01:50,112 --> 00:01:53,502
He hunted your sway for years — your
thumb-jump, up there — and never found it.

47
00:01:54,001 --> 00:01:55,510
So he did the maths on that nothing.

48
00:01:56,091 --> 00:01:58,993
To hide from his instruments, the stars
would have to sit seven hundred times

49
00:01:58,993 --> 00:02:00,050
farther out than Saturn.

50
00:02:00,630 --> 00:02:04,415
To his eye — to yours too — every bright
star shows a small round disk.

51
00:02:04,995 --> 00:02:08,804
Push a disk that wide out that far: a star
of monstrous size, dwarfing the Sun.

52
00:02:09,129 --> 00:02:10,301
Too absurd, he ruled.

53
00:02:10,882 --> 00:02:11,927
The Earth stands still.

54
00:02:12,507 --> 00:02:14,562
His logic was sound. His premise was not.

55
00:02:15,061 --> 00:02:16,211
The disks are not real.

56
00:02:16,582 --> 00:02:18,973
Air and your own eye wrap every point of
light in blur.

57
00:02:19,554 --> 00:02:20,738
The blur is what he measured.

58
00:02:20,796 --> 00:02:24,651
The largest true star disk in your night
sky is about a thousand times smaller than

59
00:02:24,651 --> 00:02:28,935
the disk he drew. It took until around
1700 to call them an illusion.

60
00:02:29,736 --> 00:02:31,965
An "optick fallacy," as it was put back
then.

61
00:02:32,232 --> 00:02:34,612
He wasn't sloppy. He measured what your
eye can see.

62
00:02:34,984 --> 00:02:36,098
It just isn't the star.

63
00:02:36,519 --> 00:02:39,085
The hunt ran on. By the 1830s it was a
race.

64
00:02:39,236 --> 00:02:42,928
Three men, three cities, three stars — and
you have met the first.

65
00:02:43,348 --> 00:02:47,609
The man in Königsberg had aimed your trick
at a dim star called 61 Cygni.

66
00:02:47,841 --> 00:02:49,884
It crawls across the sky unusually fast.

67
00:02:50,256 --> 00:02:52,624
An Italian had spotted that hurry in 1792.

68
00:02:52,949 --> 00:02:54,587
A fast star is usually a near one.

69
00:02:55,086 --> 00:02:57,338
Through 1837 and 1838 he measured.

70
00:02:57,710 --> 00:03:00,252
Then he published: a jump of about a third
of an arcsecond.

71
00:03:00,461 --> 00:03:02,238
An arcsecond: take one degree.

72
00:03:02,505 --> 00:03:04,595
Cut it into three thousand six hundred
slices.

73
00:03:04,827 --> 00:03:08,588
Keep one. His angle is a two-euro coin
seen from seventeen kilometres.

74
00:03:08,797 --> 00:03:10,469
From it he got ten point four light-years.

75
00:03:10,620 --> 00:03:12,907
A light-year is the stretch light crosses
in one year.

76
00:03:13,337 --> 00:03:14,915
Nine and a half trillion kilometres.

77
00:03:15,415 --> 00:03:17,772
Today's value for that star: eleven point
four.

78
00:03:18,097 --> 00:03:19,513
He came within a tenth of the truth.

79
00:03:19,838 --> 00:03:22,497
With a saw, a screw, and your thumb trick.

80
00:03:22,916 --> 00:03:24,135
But he wasn't first to measure.

81
00:03:24,565 --> 00:03:26,724
Go five years back, to the Cape of Good
Hope.

82
00:03:27,096 --> 00:03:30,590
A man there catches the jump of a bright
southern star, Alpha Centauri.

83
00:03:31,275 --> 00:03:33,563
He does his sums — and puts the sheet in a
drawer.

84
00:03:33,992 --> 00:03:35,397
Earlier claims had been shot down.

85
00:03:35,978 --> 00:03:38,079
The story handed down says he didn't trust
his own numbers.

86
00:03:38,404 --> 00:03:39,681
He shut the drawer on your answer.

87
00:03:39,913 --> 00:03:43,303
He published in 1839 — second, for want of
nerve.

88
00:03:43,723 --> 00:03:46,556
And in Dorpat, a third man had measured
Vega.

89
00:03:46,881 --> 00:03:51,490
He landed close: 0.125 arcseconds, against
the modern zero point

90
00:03:51,490 --> 00:03:54,462
one two nine. Then Königsberg doubted
those numbers.

91
00:03:54,834 --> 00:03:57,469
The man in Dorpat revised his good value
to nearly double.

92
00:03:57,573 --> 00:03:59,802
Away from the truth. So who was first?

93
00:03:59,895 --> 00:04:01,869
Pick your rule. First to measure: the
Cape.

94
00:04:02,368 --> 00:04:04,713
First to make the full case in print:
Königsberg.

95
00:04:05,515 --> 00:04:08,557
First to print any number at all: arguably
Dorpat.

96
00:04:08,650 --> 00:04:12,144
The books give all three answers — side by
side.

97
00:04:12,565 --> 00:04:13,784
You'd think the flood starts there.

98
00:04:13,807 --> 00:04:14,396
It doesn't.

99
00:04:14,446 --> 00:04:19,438
By 1900 — sixty years on — the world's
whole haul: about sixty parallaxes.

100
00:04:19,937 --> 00:04:22,642
Sixty known distances, in your galaxy of
billions.

101
00:04:22,968 --> 00:04:24,743
The flood came when the measuring left the
ground.

102
00:04:25,115 --> 00:04:28,807
A satellite called Hipparcos pinned down
118,000 stars in

103
00:04:28,807 --> 00:04:29,840
the 1990s.

104
00:04:30,339 --> 00:04:32,870
Only about one in six had an error under
ten percent.

105
00:04:33,799 --> 00:04:35,982
Then came Gaia, a European craft.

106
00:04:36,214 --> 00:04:40,092
From 2013 to 2025 it scanned the sky and
measured more than a billion stars.

107
00:04:40,301 --> 00:04:44,597
At its sharpest — on paper — it reads your
two-euro coin from seven hundred and

108
00:04:44,597 --> 00:04:45,967
fifty-nine thousand kilometres.

109
00:04:46,234 --> 00:04:47,395
Twice as far as the Moon.

110
00:04:47,894 --> 00:04:50,192
And still your triangle runs out inside
your own galaxy.

111
00:04:50,622 --> 00:04:53,606
The Milky Way spans some 87,000
light-years, give or take a few

112
00:04:53,606 --> 00:04:57,437
thousand. For most stars, Gaia's sharp
reach covers a slice of that.

113
00:04:57,866 --> 00:04:58,946
Build a bigger telescope.

114
00:04:59,027 --> 00:05:00,734
The base of your triangle has not grown a
metre.

115
00:05:00,908 --> 00:05:02,998
Past a point, the jump is smaller than the
blur.

116
00:05:03,418 --> 00:05:08,434
Everything farther — every other galaxy,
the deep sky — stands on something else.

117
00:05:08,503 --> 00:05:12,938
A ladder. Its second rung was built by a
woman paid thirty cents an hour.

118
00:05:13,359 --> 00:05:14,903
Step into a long bright workroom.

119
00:05:15,228 --> 00:05:17,260
Harvard, 1908 to 1912.

120
00:05:17,840 --> 00:05:20,499
At the tables sit women, bent over the sky
on glass.

121
00:05:20,929 --> 00:05:23,158
The plates come from the men who run the
telescopes.

122
00:05:23,587 --> 00:05:25,967
The women measure them. Stop at one table.

123
00:05:26,467 --> 00:05:30,611
Under her magnifier lies the Small
Magellanic Cloud, a companion galaxy of

124
00:05:30,983 --> 00:05:32,992
Plate after plate, she finds stars that
pulse.

125
00:05:33,317 --> 00:05:35,255
They brighten, fade, and brighten again.

126
00:05:35,685 --> 00:05:37,148
A steady beat of days or weeks.

127
00:05:37,194 --> 00:05:38,657
We now call them Cepheids.

128
00:05:39,087 --> 00:05:40,457
She works through twenty-five of them.

129
00:05:40,631 --> 00:05:42,720
And the pattern rises out of the glass in
front of you.

130
00:05:43,046 --> 00:05:45,101
The slower the beat, the brighter the
star.

131
00:05:45,681 --> 00:05:48,235
On her chart, the points fall along two
clean straight lines.

132
00:05:48,665 --> 00:05:50,314
She writes down, herself, what it means.

133
00:05:50,523 --> 00:05:54,191
These stars all sit at roughly the same
distance from us — they share the Cloud.

134
00:05:54,691 --> 00:05:58,127
So the beat apparently tracks how much
light a star truly puts out.

135
00:05:58,547 --> 00:05:59,638
Hold on to what she wrote.

136
00:06:00,068 --> 00:06:01,728
It unlocks the deep universe for you.

137
00:06:02,053 --> 00:06:05,536
Read a Cepheid's rhythm, and you know its
true brightness.

138
00:06:06,117 --> 00:06:08,961
See how faint it looks, and the dimming
hands you the distance.

139
00:06:09,460 --> 00:06:11,782
Hers you could read across millions of
light-years.

140
00:06:12,202 --> 00:06:14,094
One catch — and she named it too.

141
00:06:14,524 --> 00:06:15,662
Her chart had no scale.

142
00:06:16,091 --> 00:06:19,957
Nobody knew the distance to the Cloud
itself, so her law gave only ratios.

143
00:06:20,224 --> 00:06:22,326
This star is four times farther than that
one.

144
00:06:23,011 --> 00:06:24,011
Four times what?

145
00:06:24,299 --> 00:06:27,562
She wrote that she hoped parallaxes would
be measured for a few stars of this kind.

146
00:06:27,933 --> 00:06:30,128
A year on, an astronomer found a rough
scale.

147
00:06:30,499 --> 00:06:35,329
No clean triangle — the slow drift of
thirteen nearby Cepheids, pooled.

148
00:06:35,749 --> 00:06:37,653
Her paper went out under her director's
signature.

149
00:06:37,978 --> 00:06:40,950
Her name survives in the first line:
"prepared by Miss Leavitt".

150
00:06:41,751 --> 00:06:45,327
In 1925, a Swedish mathematician wrote to
her about putting her name up for the

151
00:06:45,327 --> 00:06:48,566
Nobel Prize. He did not know she had been
dead for four years.

152
00:06:48,987 --> 00:06:50,694
Mark the shape of what you now own.

153
00:06:51,019 --> 00:06:52,389
Your triangle is the bottom rung.

154
00:06:52,656 --> 00:06:54,618
The Cepheids stand on the triangle.

155
00:06:54,885 --> 00:06:59,448
The rungs above — exploding stars, whole
galaxies — stand on the Cepheids.

156
00:06:59,878 --> 00:07:02,966
Every rung inherits the reach of the one
below — and its errors.

157
00:07:03,385 --> 00:07:06,264
And the errors came. Your ladder has
snapped twice.

158
00:07:06,684 --> 00:07:07,644
First break.

159
00:07:07,694 --> 00:07:13,197
In 1929, a man at a great telescope in
California used Cepheid distances to show

160
00:07:13,197 --> 00:07:14,637
the galaxies flee from us.

161
00:07:14,904 --> 00:07:16,053
The farther, the faster.

162
00:07:16,425 --> 00:07:17,667
The universe expands.

163
00:07:18,166 --> 00:07:20,303
Run that film backwards, and you get an
age.

164
00:07:20,628 --> 00:07:22,636
His rate gave: not quite two billion
years.

165
00:07:23,008 --> 00:07:26,607
Awkward, even then. The geologists put the
Earth itself at around two billion.

166
00:07:26,932 --> 00:07:28,534
By the mid-fifties it turned absurd.

167
00:07:29,115 --> 00:07:31,205
The Earth's true age came in at four and a
half billion.

168
00:07:31,437 --> 00:07:34,966
Run those numbers, and you are standing on
a planet older than the universe around

169
00:07:34,966 --> 00:07:37,823
it. Part of the fault lay on Leavitt's
rung.

170
00:07:38,252 --> 00:07:41,317
There are two families of pulsing stars,
with two different rulers.

171
00:07:42,118 --> 00:07:43,651
Mixing them had shrunk the cosmos.

172
00:07:44,150 --> 00:07:47,807
In 1952, in a hall in Rome, a man stood up
and pulled the families apart.

173
00:07:48,178 --> 00:07:49,792
At a stroke, the universe doubled.

174
00:07:50,164 --> 00:07:51,164
Still too small.

175
00:07:51,232 --> 00:07:55,563
In 1958, another astronomer found: the
California man had also mistaken glowing

176
00:07:55,563 --> 00:07:56,759
clouds for bright stars.

177
00:07:57,188 --> 00:08:00,393
The rate fell again, and the universe came
out comfortably older than the Earth.

178
00:08:00,660 --> 00:08:03,760
Total correction, first to last: about a
factor of seven.

179
00:08:04,179 --> 00:08:05,607
Second break, closer to home.

180
00:08:05,688 --> 00:08:08,811
The Pleiades — the little cluster you can
spot with your bare eyes.

181
00:08:09,078 --> 00:08:13,095
Hipparcos, the trusted satellite, put it
at about 390 light-years.

182
00:08:13,467 --> 00:08:16,358
Nearly every other method said 435 to 445.

183
00:08:16,625 --> 00:08:19,574
For seventeen years, the field's best
instrument disagreed with everyone else.

184
00:08:19,899 --> 00:08:21,780
About one of the nearest clusters in your
sky.

185
00:08:22,105 --> 00:08:26,447
In 2014, radio telescopes settled it: 444
light-years, give or take four.

186
00:08:26,714 --> 00:08:27,863
The satellite was wrong.

187
00:08:28,283 --> 00:08:31,116
And today your ladder is in its third
fight — this one still open.

188
00:08:31,441 --> 00:08:34,100
Two teams, two numbers for how fast the
universe grows.

189
00:08:34,529 --> 00:08:36,340
One number comes from the oldest light
there is.

190
00:08:36,665 --> 00:08:40,380
A satellite called Planck reads 67.4, with
an error of half a

191
00:08:40,380 --> 00:08:42,284
point. The other comes off your ladder.

192
00:08:42,969 --> 00:08:46,801
Parallax, to Cepheids, to exploding stars
that all flare to nearly the same true

193
00:08:46,801 --> 00:08:47,784
brightness.

194
00:08:47,834 --> 00:08:51,491
The ladder team, called SH0ES, reads 73.0,
plus or minus one.

195
00:08:51,642 --> 00:08:54,870
A five-sigma difference, in their own
words: far too large to be chance.

196
00:08:55,195 --> 00:08:57,540
You'll hear that sold as the early
universe against today's.

197
00:08:58,341 --> 00:08:59,792
Watch what that framing skips.

198
00:08:59,850 --> 00:09:02,288
A second team climbed the same ladder with
a different second rung.

199
00:09:02,787 --> 00:09:06,897
Red giant stars instead of Cepheids, the
new James Webb telescope in the mix.

200
00:09:07,327 --> 00:09:10,601
They read 67.8 to 70.4, depending on the
sample

201
00:09:10,601 --> 00:09:13,399
and the method. Their own verdict: no new
physics needed.

202
00:09:13,550 --> 00:09:17,102
Strangest of all: both teams agree on the
distances to the very same galaxies.

203
00:09:17,601 --> 00:09:18,623
To about one percent.

204
00:09:18,948 --> 00:09:20,156
The stars are not the quarrel.

205
00:09:20,736 --> 00:09:23,488
The quarrel is over which exploding stars
to hang the scale on.

206
00:09:24,068 --> 00:09:26,332
The ladder holds. The fight is about the
nail.

207
00:09:26,460 --> 00:09:28,190
Nobody yet knows which side is right.

208
00:09:28,611 --> 00:09:30,503
Go back out to your street and find your
star.

209
00:09:30,829 --> 00:09:33,975
How far away is it? For the near ones:
your triangle.

210
00:09:34,555 --> 00:09:36,924
The thumb trick, stretched across your
planet's orbit.

211
00:09:37,504 --> 00:09:40,024
Sharpened until a coin past the Moon is an
easy target.

212
00:09:40,605 --> 00:09:42,079
For the far ones: the ladder.

213
00:09:42,404 --> 00:09:44,575
A rhythm read off glass plates at thirty
cents an hour.

214
00:09:44,947 --> 00:09:48,941
Nailed to your triangle. Broken twice,
patched twice, fought over right now.

215
00:09:49,626 --> 00:09:52,655
One in four of the people asked still
hangs the stars closer than the Sun.

216
00:09:52,806 --> 00:09:55,999
The nearest one sits so deep that its
light spends four years on the road to

217
00:09:55,999 --> 00:09:58,101
eye. But that number is no guess.

218
00:09:58,472 --> 00:10:00,957
It never was. Someone caught its jump.

219
00:10:01,537 --> 00:10:02,594
Someone read its beat.

220
00:10:03,279 --> 00:10:05,787
And beside every distance, someone wrote a
second number.

221
00:10:06,286 --> 00:10:07,354
How far off it might be.

222
00:10:07,935 --> 00:10:09,514
That second number is the honest answer.

223
00:10:09,885 --> 00:10:11,650
You don't just know how far your star is.

224
00:10:12,335 --> 00:10:15,052
You know how well you know it — and
exactly where you don't.
